        <body>
  <h2>Requesting a Special Education Evaluation in Brentwood UFSD: A Parent's Guide</h2>

  <section>
    <h3>Local Context: Brentwood UFSD and Special Education</h3>
    <p>Brentwood Union Free School District (UFSD), located in Brentwood, Suffolk County, is the largest non-city school district in New York State. Serving neighborhoods across Brentwood and parts of neighboring communities, the district has a significant student population and correspondingly high Individualized Education Program (IEP) caseload. Brentwood UFSD is committed to providing comprehensive special education services, including bilingual evaluations in Spanish, to meet the diverse needs of its students. Navigating the special education system can be daunting. This guide provides parents with a comprehensive overview of how to request an evaluation, understand your rights, and advocate effectively for your child within Brentwood UFSD.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h3>Initiating the Process: The Written Referral - Your First Step</h3>
    <p>The formal process for obtaining a special education evaluation in Brentwood UFSD begins with a written referral.  This is a crucial first step, and the clarity and detail of your referral can significantly impact the evaluation process. This letter officially informs the district that you suspect your child may have a disability that is impacting their education and requires special education services and/or related services.  According to New York State Education Law §4402 and Part 200 of the Regulations of the Commissioner of Education, parents have the right to request an evaluation at any time. The district <i>must</i> act upon this request.</p>

    <p>To formally request a special education evaluation for your child in Brentwood UFSD, you must submit a written referral to the Director of Special Education (or the designated point of contact for special education referrals in the district). This referral should clearly state your concerns about your child's academic, behavioral, or developmental progress. Be as specific as possible, outlining specific challenges observed at home and/or in school. Include any supporting documentation you have, such as report cards, teacher communications, or medical records. The more comprehensive your initial referral, the better the district can understand your concerns and tailor the evaluation process appropriately.</p>

    <p><b>Essential Elements of Your Written Request:</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Your child's full legal name and date of birth:</b> Ensure accuracy for proper record-keeping.</li>
      <li><b>Your child's current grade level and school:</b> This helps the district route the referral to the correct school psychologist and CSE team.</li>
      <li><b>A detailed description of your concerns:</b> This is the most critical part of your referral.  Don't just say "My child is struggling in reading."  Instead, say something like, "My child is currently in 3rd grade and is reading at a late 1st-grade level.  They struggle with decoding multi-syllable words, frequently guess at words instead of sounding them out, and become frustrated when asked to read aloud.  This impacts their comprehension of written material and their overall academic performance."  Include specific examples of behaviors, academic challenges, or developmental delays.  Consider including examples of homework assignments, test scores, or behavioral incidents.</li>
      <li><b>Specific examples of how the issues impact educational performance:</b> Crucially, connect the issues to the child's ability to learn and progress in the general education curriculum. For example, "Because of [issue], my child is unable to complete grade-level writing assignments and is falling behind in all subjects that require written output."</li>
      <li><b>Any interventions or strategies that have already been tried:</b> Detail any interventions implemented by the school or at home, and their level of success. This demonstrates that you and the school have already attempted to address the issues.  For instance, "The teacher has implemented small group reading instruction with [specific program], but my child continues to struggle.  We have also tried extra reading practice at home using [specific method], but we have seen minimal improvement."</li>
      <li><b>Contact information for yourself (phone number and email address):</b>  Provide reliable contact information for prompt communication.</li>
      <li><b>Your signature and the date:</b> This authenticates your request.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Model Referral Letter Snippet:</b></p>
    <p><i>"To Whom It May Concern,</i></p>
    <p><i>I am writing to request a special education evaluation for my child, [Child's Full Name], DOB: [Date of Birth], who is currently in [Grade Level] at [School Name]. I have significant concerns regarding [Child's Name]'s progress in mathematics. Specifically, [he/she] struggles with [specific math skill, e.g., multi-digit multiplication, understanding fractions]. Despite receiving extra help from the teacher and completing all assigned homework, [Child's Name] continues to perform significantly below grade level on math assessments. This difficulty is impacting [his/her] ability to participate in class and is causing [him/her] significant anxiety..."</i></p>

    <p>Contact information for the Director of Special Education can be found on the Brentwood UFSD website or by calling the district office. Keep a copy of your referral letter for your records, and send it via certified mail with return receipt requested. This provides proof that the district received your request. This is extremely important for tracking purposes and holding the district accountable to the 60-day timeline.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h3>Understanding the 60-Day Timeline:  A Critical Deadline</h3>
    <p>New York State Education Law and IDEA (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act) mandate specific timelines for the special education evaluation process. Once Brentwood UFSD receives your written referral, they have a legal obligation to complete the evaluation process within 60 <b>school</b> days (as defined in NYS Part 200.1(ww)). This timeline is measured from the date the district *receives* your written consent for the evaluation to the date the IEP is finalized. This includes obtaining parental consent for the evaluations, conducting the evaluations, holding a meeting of the Committee on Special Education (CSE), and determining eligibility for special education services. </p>

    <p><b>Breaking Down the 60-Day Timeline:</b></p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Day 1:</b> District receives your written referral AND parental consent to evaluate.  The 60-day clock starts ticking.  <b>Important:</b> The district *cannot* begin the evaluation process until they have your signed consent. Make sure you provide this consent promptly after submitting your referral.</li>
        <li><b>Evaluation Planning:</b> The school psychologist, along with other relevant professionals (e.g., speech therapist, occupational therapist, educational evaluator), will determine the appropriate evaluations to administer. This should be tailored to the specific concerns outlined in your referral.  Common evaluations include cognitive assessments (IQ tests), academic achievement tests, and behavioral assessments.</li>
        <li><b>Evaluation Administration:</b> The evaluations are administered by qualified professionals. You have the right to request copies of the evaluation reports before the CSE meeting.  According to IDEA, you are entitled to all evaluation data.</li>
        <li><b>CSE Meeting Scheduling:</b> The district must provide you with sufficient notice of the CSE meeting (usually at least 5 days). The notice should include the purpose of the meeting, the names of the participants, and your right to invite others who have knowledge or special expertise regarding your child.</li>
        <li><b>CSE Meeting and Eligibility Determination:</b> The CSE meets to review the evaluation results, discuss your concerns, and determine whether your child meets the criteria for a disability under IDEA and New York State regulations.  If your child is found eligible, the CSE will develop an Individualized Education Program (IEP).</li>
        <li><b>IEP Development:</b> The IEP outlines your child's present levels of performance, measurable goals, and the specific special education services and supports they will receive. You have the right to participate fully in the IEP development process.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><b>What to Do if the 60-Day Timeline is Not Met:</b></p>
    <p>It is crucial to understand this timeline and to proactively follow up with the district if you haven't heard from them within a reasonable timeframe. Document <i>all</i> communication with the district regarding your request – dates, times, names of individuals you spoke with, and the content of the conversation.  Be aware that delays can sometimes occur due to the high volume of requests in a large district like Brentwood UFSD, so consistent communication is key. If the district fails to meet the 60-day timeline, you have several options:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Contact the Director of Special Education:</b>  Express your concerns in writing and reiterate the importance of adhering to the legal timeline. Request a written explanation for the delay and a firm commitment to a revised timeline.</li>
        <li><b>File a Formal Complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED):</b> You can file a complaint alleging that the district has violated IDEA regulations. NYSED will investigate the complaint and issue a decision. Instructions on how to file a complaint can be found on the NYSED website.</li>
        <li><b>Request Mediation:</b> Mediation is a voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the district reach a mutually agreeable resolution.</li>
        <li><b>Due Process Complaint:</b> This is a more formal legal process that involves a hearing before an impartial hearing officer. Due process is generally used when mediation fails, or when the issues are complex and require legal intervention.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>Advocacy Tip:</b>  Calendar the 60-day deadline and set reminders to follow up with the district at regular intervals (e.g., every two weeks).  Maintain a detailed log of all communication.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
      <h3>Parental Rights During the Evaluation Process</h3>
      <p>Parents have specific rights throughout the special education evaluation process, as outlined in IDEA and New York State regulations (Part 200). It is important to be aware of these rights to effectively advocate for your child.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><b>Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</b> If you disagree with the results of the district's evaluation, you have the right to obtain an IEE at public expense (meaning the district pays for it).  You must demonstrate why you disagree with the district's evaluation. The district can either agree to pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to defend their evaluation. If the district prevails at the due process hearing, you still have the right to an IEE, but you must pay for it yourself.</li>
          <li><b>Access to Records:</b> You have the right to inspect and review all of your child's educational records, including evaluation reports, IEPs, and progress reports.</li>
          <li><b>Prior Written Notice:</b> The district must provide you with prior written notice whenever they propose to initiate or change, or refuse to initiate or change, the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of your child. This notice must be in your native language and must explain the reasons for the district's actions.</li>
          <li><b>Parental Consent:</b> The district must obtain your written consent before conducting an initial evaluation, before implementing an IEP, and before making any changes to your child's placement.</li>
          <li><b>Participation in CSE Meetings:</b> You have the right to participate fully in all CSE meetings and to bring anyone you choose to the meeting, including advocates, attorneys, and experts.</li>
      </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
      <h3>Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them in Brentwood UFSD</h3>
      <p>While Brentwood UFSD is committed to providing special education services, parents may encounter certain challenges during the evaluation process. Here are some common pitfalls and strategies for avoiding them:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><b>Delays in the Evaluation Process:</b> As mentioned earlier, delays are a common issue in large districts.  Be proactive in following up with the district and documenting all communication. If delays persist, consider filing a complaint with NYSED.</li>
          <li><b>Inadequate Evaluations:</b> Ensure that the evaluations are comprehensive and address all of your concerns. If you believe the evaluations are insufficient, request additional evaluations or an IEE. For example, if your child's primary issues are in writing, ensure the psychoeducational evaluation includes a comprehensive written language assessment.</li>
          <li><b>Predetermined Outcomes:</b> Be wary of situations where it seems the district has already decided your child's eligibility or placement before the evaluation process is complete. The evaluation should drive the decisions, not the other way around.</li>
          <li><b>Lack of Communication:</b> Maintain open and frequent communication with the school psychologist, teachers, and other members of the CSE team. Address any concerns promptly and in writing.</li>
          <li><b>IEP Implementation Issues:</b> Once an IEP is developed, ensure that it is being implemented correctly and consistently. Monitor your child's progress and address any concerns with the CSE team.</li>
      </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>
      <dl>
          <dt><b>Q: What if my child is already receiving interventions in the general education classroom? Do I still need to request an evaluation?</b></dt>
          <dd>A: Yes. While interventions are helpful, they are not a substitute for a formal special education evaluation. If you suspect your child has a disability that is impacting their educational performance, you have the right to request an evaluation, regardless of whether they are receiving interventions. The evaluation will determine if the child requires specialized instruction and support beyond what can be provided in the general education setting. Also, document these interventions in your referral letter.</dd>

          <dt><b>Q: Can the school refuse to evaluate my child?</b></dt>
          <dd>A: The school can refuse to evaluate your child *if* they provide you with prior written notice explaining their reasons for refusing and the data upon which that refusal is based. You then have the right to challenge that decision through mediation or due process.  It is relatively rare for a school to outright refuse an evaluation request. More often, the challenge lies in ensuring the evaluation is comprehensive and timely.</dd>

          <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the IEP that the CSE develops?</b></dt>
          <dd>A: You have several options if you disagree with the IEP. You can request another CSE meeting to discuss your concerns. You can also pursue mediation or file a due process complaint. You can also implement the IEP while simultaneously pursuing other options, to ensure your child is receiving some services while the dispute is being resolved. It's best to consult with a special education attorney or advocate to understand your options and develop a strategy.</dd>

          <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between an IEP and a 504 plan?</b></dt>
          <dd>A: An IEP is for students who have been identified as having a disability that requires specialized instruction. A 504 plan is for students who have a disability that substantially limits one or more major life activities, but who do not require specialized instruction. 504 plans provide accommodations to help students access the general education curriculum. The requirements for evaluations, parental consent, and procedural safeguards are much stronger under IDEA (IEPs) than under Section 504.</dd>

          <dt><b>Q: My child speaks Spanish. Will the evaluation be conducted in Spanish?</b></dt>
          <dd>A: Yes. According to IDEA and New York State regulations, evaluations must be conducted in the child's native language or mode of communication. Brentwood UFSD is required to provide bilingual evaluations in Spanish for students who are Spanish speakers. Be sure to specifically request a bilingual evaluation in your referral letter if your child's primary language is Spanish.</dd>
      </dl>
  </section>

</body>
